course: world
lesson: 76
title: Trees and forests through the year
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A tree is a large plant with a woody trunk; each year it grows a new layer of wood under its bark, which we can see as a ring when the tree is cut.
explain: Deciduous trees, like oak, maple and birch, drop their leaves in autumn and rest through winter; evergreen trees, like pine and spruce, keep their needles all year and lose old ones a few at a time.
explain: In autumn the tree stops making green chlorophyll, so the yellow and orange colours that were hidden in the leaf show through; in spring new buds open and the tree starts making food again.
explain: A forest has layers: tall trees make the canopy, smaller trees and bushes grow beneath, and the forest floor is covered with fallen leaves that rot and feed the soil.
explain: A wide ring in a cut trunk means a good year with plenty of rain and warmth; a thin ring means a dry or cold year — so a stump is like a diary of the tree's life.
fact: A tree usually grows one new ring of wood each year.
fact: Counting the rings in a stump tells us roughly how old the tree was.
fact: Deciduous trees lose all their leaves in autumn.
fact: Dropping their leaves helps deciduous trees save water in winter, when the ground is frozen and the roots can take up little water.
fact: Evergreen trees keep leaves all year and lose old ones little by little.
fact: Leaves turn yellow and orange in autumn because the green chlorophyll breaks down.
fact: Thin needles with a waxy coat help evergreen trees lose little water in winter.
fact: The canopy is the top layer of a forest, made by the tallest trees.
fact: Fallen leaves are broken down by worms, fungi and bacteria, and this feeds the soil.
fact: Trees take in carbon dioxide and give out oxygen when they make food.
fact: Tree roots hold the soil together and stop it from washing away.
fact: Some trees, such as bristlecone pines, can live for more than 4,000 years.
exercise: What can we count to find the age of a cut tree? | The rings in its trunk | The branches on its top | The leaves on the ground | The cracks in its bark
exercise: What do deciduous trees do in autumn? | They drop their leaves | They grow new leaves | They turn their needles blue | They pull their roots up
exercise: Which tree is evergreen? | Pine | Oak | Maple | Birch
exercise: Why do leaves turn yellow and orange in autumn? | The green chlorophyll breaks down and hidden colours show | The cold paints them with new colours | The sunlight burns them at the edges | The rain washes the green colour out of them in the autumn
exercise: What is the canopy of a forest? | The top layer made by the tallest trees | The soil under the thick layer of fallen leaves | The roots that grow deep down underground | The small plants and mosses on the ground
exercise: What happens to fallen leaves on the forest floor? | They rot and feed the soil | They stay the same for ever | They float up back to the branches | They turn into stones over winter
exercise: Who breaks down fallen leaves? | Worms, fungi and bacteria | Birds and squirrels only | The wind and the moon | Nobody, they just disappear
exercise: How do evergreen trees lose their old needles? | A few at a time all year | All at once in autumn | Never, needles live for ever | Only when the tree dies
exercise: What does a wide tree ring tell us? | That year the tree grew well | That year was very dry and hot | The tree was cut that year | The tree was ill that year
exercise: Why do deciduous trees drop their leaves before winter? | To save water when the ground is frozen | Because the leaves get too heavy with snow | To make room for fruit to grow in winter | Because hungry birds eat them all in autumn
exercise: How do roots help the land? | They hold the soil together | They make the rain fall | They warm the ground in winter | They push stones out of the soil
exercise: What gas do trees give out when they make food? | Oxygen | Carbon dioxide | Smoke | Steam
exercise: What happens to a tree's buds in spring? | They open into new leaves and flowers | They fall off the tree along with the snow | They turn into sharp green needles instead | They grow downwards and become new roots
exercise: How do thin waxy needles help a pine in winter? | They lose very little water | They catch more snow to drink | They keep the bark warm | They scare away animals
test: A stump has 35 rings. About how old was the tree? | About 35 years old | About 3 years old | About 350 years old | We cannot tell from rings
test: In January, a park has bare trees and green trees. Which are evergreen? | The green ones | The bare ones | Both kinds | Neither kind
test: Which is true about autumn leaves? | Their yellow colour was there all summer, hidden by green | Their colour comes from cold frost | They turn red because the sun is closer | They change colour because the trees are dying slowly in the cold
test: A stump has a few very thin rings in a row. What probably happened in those years? | Dry or cold years with poor growth | Years with lots of rain and sun | The tree grew extra tall then | The tree was a different kind then
test: What would happen to forest soil if no leaves ever rotted? | It would get poorer, with no new food for plants | It would become richer every year as the leaves stayed on top | Nothing would change at all | Trees would grow faster
test: Why is it hard for small plants to grow under a thick canopy? | The tall trees block most of the light | The canopy makes the soil too salty | The canopy keeps all the rain for itself | The tall trees eat the small plants
test: A hillside lost all its trees. What is likely to happen in heavy rain? | Soil will wash away down the hill | The soil will hold more water | The hill will grow new rocks | Nothing, trees do not affect soil
test: Which tree keeps its leaves green through a snowy winter? | Spruce | Oak | Birch | Maple
test: Why is a tree stump like a diary? | Its rings record each year's growth | It writes the names of animals | It keeps leaves from every year | It shows the weather of tomorrow
test: why do leaves go all orange in autumn | The green chlorophyll breaks down, so other colours show | The cold paints the leaves orange overnight | The tree fills the leaves with orange juice | The sun burns the leaves at the end of summer
test: how'd u know how old that big tree stump is | Count the rings, about one ring for each year | Measure how wide the stump is in metres | Count the branches that grew from the trunk | Look at how dark the bark has gone
test: Ava says pine trees never lose any leaves. true? | No, evergreens lose old needles little by little | Yes, evergreen needles last for the tree's whole life | Yes, but only if they grow in warm places | No, pine trees drop all their needles in autumn
test: my brother reckons trees breathe in oxygen and give out carbon dioxide when they make food. right? | No, when they make food they take in carbon dioxide and give out oxygen | Yes, trees make food from oxygen in the air | Yes, but only at night when it is dark | No, trees make food without any gases at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the means of estimating the age of a felled tree? | Counting the rings of wood in its stump | Measuring the height of its trunk | Counting the branches upon its crown | Weighing the leaves which it bears
exam: Identify the principal reason for which leaves turn yellow and orange in autumn. | The green chlorophyll breaks down | The leaves absorb the colour of the sunset | Frost paints the leaves with colour | The tree draws water into the leaves
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning evergreen trees. | They retain leaves all year, losing old ones little by little | They lose all their leaves each autumn | They bear no leaves of any kind | They lose all their leaves each spring
exam: Ascertain the gases exchanged by trees in the manufacture of their food. | Carbon dioxide is taken in and oxygen given out | Oxygen is taken in and carbon dioxide given out | Nitrogen is taken in and oxygen given out | Oxygen is taken in and nitrogen given out
